# DOORS → Neo4j Importer — Specification

**Status:** design agreed, ready for implementation
**Target:** Neo4j 2026.01.4 **Community Edition**, Cypher 25
**Input:** JSON files produced by the `doors-exporter` DXL script

---

## 0. Purpose and scope

Build an application that imports IBM DOORS modules and their objects into a Neo4j
graph, preserving hierarchy and traceability links, so that later functionality can
analyse the requirements and — in a future phase — join them to data from other
Systems Engineering sources (MBSE tools, test management, PLM, …).

**In scope now:** parse the DXL JSON export, model it as a graph, import it
idempotently, validate the result, report anomalies.

**Explicitly out of scope now** (but the model must not preclude them):

- Baseline-to-current comparison / version chaining.
- Link-module semantics (satisfies / verifies / refines). All DOORS links become one
  relationship type.
- Non-DOORS data sources.
- Any write-back to DOORS.

---

## 1. Ground rules that drive the whole design

These are the decisions everything else follows from. Do not deviate without asking.

| # | Rule |
|---|---|
| R1 | `__id` is **application-level metadata**, not the source system's identifier. Every node in the database has `__id`, `__name`, `__version`. For DOORS nodes, `__id` **is the DOORS URL, verbatim**. |
| R2 | All writes are `MERGE` on `__id` — "update if exists, otherwise create". Importing the same file twice must produce an identical graph. |
| R3 | Only `__outputLinks` are imported. `__inputLinks` are **ignored entirely**. |
| R4 | `__child` connects **direct** parent to **direct** child only. A module links to its level-1 objects; a level-6 heading links to its level-7 children. There is no module→level-7 shortcut. |
| R5 | A link target that is not yet in the database becomes a placeholder node labelled `__UNDEFINED`, carrying only what can be derived from the link. It is upgraded in place when the real object is imported. |
| R6 | **No placeholder modules.** If a link points into a module that has not been imported, only the placeholder *object* is created. It stays parentless until its module is imported. |
| R7 | Hierarchy is derived from `objectNumber` by **splitting on `.`**, never by string prefix matching. |
| R8 | DOORS URLs are used **verbatim** — no normalisation, no case folding, no query-parameter reordering. Trim whitespace only. |

---

## 2. Input format

### 2.1 File shape

One JSON file per module export. UTF-8. Single top-level object.

```jsonc
{
  "__objectId":     "000969a2",          // qualifiedUniqueID of the module
  "__name":         "SRD",
  "__version":      "current",           // or a baseline version string, e.g. "4.0"
  "description":    "…",
  "moduleFullPath": "/XXX-/Level 1 - System/SRD",
  "url":            "doors://…-M-000969a2",

  // ── formal module attributes, names transformed by the DXL ──
  "prefix": "SRD-", "created_By": "…", "created_On": "…",
  "last_Modified_By": "…", "last_Modified_On": "…", "_ModuleType": "Customer",
  "wordDocBaseline": "4.0", "wordDocCaptionLevel": "2", "wordDocIssue": "07",
  "wordDocNumber": "…", "wordDocTitle": "…",

  "__contents": [ /* objects */ ]
}
```

> The module-attribute names are produced by `lowerCase(replace(name, " ", "_"))`,
> which lowercases **only the first character**. Hence `created_By`, not `created_by`.
> `_ModuleType` keeps its leading underscore because `lowerCase` leaves `_` untouched.
> Treat all of these as opaque pass-through properties.

### 2.2 Object shape

Each entry of `__contents` has **10 fixed metadata keys plus every visible DOORS
attribute of that module**. The attribute set differs from module to module — the
reference file has 78 of them, all module-specific.

```jsonc
{
  "__tableObject":      "false",         // string "true" / "false"
  "__tableID":          "",              // DOORS identifier of the table, e.g. "SRD-998"
  "__tableURL":         "",              // resource URL of the table object
  "__tableRowIndex":    "",              // string integer
  "__tableColumnIndex": "",              // string integer
  "id":                 "SRD-1",         // DOORS identifier, unique within module only
  "objectNumber":       "1",
  "objectLevel":        "1",             // string integer
  "__moduleUrl":        "doors://…-M-000969a2",

  "Object Text": "…", "Object Heading": "…", "Object Type": "…", /* …75 more… */

  "__objectUrl":   "doors://…-O-1-000969a2",
  "__outputLinks": [ { "reqDocumentURL": "doors://…-M-0009630f", "absoluteNumber": "95" } ],
  "__inputLinks":  [ /* same shape — IGNORED, see R3 */ ]
}
```

**Every value is a string.** There are no JSON numbers or booleans anywhere except the
two link arrays. Cast deliberately; do not assume.

### 2.3 URL grammar

Verified against the reference export: the prefix
`doors://doors.company.corp:9601/?version=2&prodID=0&urn=urn:telelogic::1-0000000000000000`
is **byte-identical** across all 984 objects and all 6 referenced modules.
`version=2` is the DOORS *URL schema* version — it is **not** a baseline indicator.

| | Module | Object |
|---|---|---|
| Current | `<prefix>-M-<modId>` | `<prefix>-O-<absNo>-<modId>` |
| Baseline | `<prefix>-B-<modId>-<versionId>` | `<prefix>-V-<absNo>-<modId>-<versionId>` |

`<versionId>` is the same string that appears in the module's `__version`.

**Deriving a link target's URL.** Always derive by **substitution on the module URL**,
never by parsing an object URL right-to-left. `<versionId>` may itself contain `-`,
which makes right-to-left tokenising unsafe; substitution is immune.

```python
def target_object_url(req_document_url: str, absolute_number: str) -> str:
    u = req_document_url.strip()
    if "-M-" in u:
        head, mod_id = u.rsplit("-M-", 1)
        return f"{head}-O-{absolute_number}-{mod_id}"
    if "-B-" in u:
        head, rest = u.rsplit("-B-", 1)          # rest = "<modId>-<versionId>"
        return f"{head}-V-{absolute_number}-{rest}"
    raise MalformedUrlError(u)
```

The same function derives `__version` for a placeholder: `-M-` → `"current"`,
`-B-` → the `<versionId>` portion.

