## 7. Neo4j specifics — 2026.01.4 Community Edition

### 7.1 Cypher version

From Neo4j 2026.02 the shipped `neo4j.conf` sets `db.query.default_language=CYPHER_25`.
**2026.01.4 is one release before that flip**, so the server default depends on how the
database was created. Therefore: **prefix every statement with `CYPHER 25`** so behaviour
is deterministic regardless of server configuration.

### 7.2 Constraints — Community Edition supports uniqueness only

Property-existence constraints, key constraints (uniqueness + existence combined),
relationship-property constraints and property-type constraints are **Enterprise-only**.
Single-property and composite uniqueness constraints are available in Community.

```cypher
CYPHER 25
CREATE CONSTRAINT se_item_id_unique IF NOT EXISTS
FOR (n:SEItem) REQUIRE n.__id IS UNIQUE;

CYPHER 25
CREATE CONSTRAINT doors_object_url_unique IF NOT EXISTS
FOR (n:DOORSObject) REQUIRE n.__objectUrl IS UNIQUE;
```

`__objectUrl` currently holds the same value as `__id`. Keep both: `__objectUrl` is the
source-native field, `__id` is the application key. A future MBSE source will derive
`__id` from its own `uuid` and will have no `__objectUrl` at all — that separation is the
whole point of R1.

**Consequence:** "every node has `__name` and `__version`" cannot be enforced by the
database. It is enforced by the importer and re-checked by the validation queries in §8.

### 7.3 Indexes

```cypher
CYPHER 25 CREATE INDEX doors_object_id       IF NOT EXISTS FOR (n:DOORSObject) ON (n.id);
CYPHER 25 CREATE INDEX doors_object_module   IF NOT EXISTS FOR (n:DOORSObject) ON (n.__moduleUrl);
CYPHER 25 CREATE INDEX doors_object_sortkey  IF NOT EXISTS FOR (n:DOORSObject) ON (n.__sortKey);
CYPHER 25 CREATE INDEX se_item_name          IF NOT EXISTS FOR (n:SEItem)      ON (n.__name);
```

Do not create an index on `__id` or `__objectUrl` — the uniqueness constraints already
create backing range indexes, and creating a duplicate index will error.

Note that `id` is **only unique within a module**, so it must never be used as a key.

### 7.4 Writing properties — use map parameters, not literal keys

DOORS attribute names contain spaces, dots, slashes, parentheses and non-ASCII
characters. Writing them as literal Cypher property keys would require backtick quoting
and careful escaping.

**Avoid the problem entirely:** pass a map parameter and use `SET n += row.props`.
Property keys supplied via a map need no quoting.

```cypher
CYPHER 25
UNWIND $rows AS row
MERGE (n:SEItem {__id: row.__id})
REMOVE n:__UNDEFINED
SET n:DOORSObject:DOORSRequirement
SET n += row.props
```

Never build Cypher by string concatenation from DOORS data. The only string-built part of
any statement is the static label list chosen from the closed set in §5.1.

### 7.5 Batching

- Input is JSON and app-driven, so **do not use `LOAD CSV`**. Use `UNWIND $rows` with
  driver-side batching.
- Batch size 1 000–5 000 rows per transaction; make it configurable. Start at 1 000 —
  object rows are wide (88+ properties).
- Use `session.execute_write()` with the driver's built-in retry, one transaction per
  batch, so a failure retries only that batch.
- `CALL () { … } IN TRANSACTIONS OF n ROWS` is available in Cypher 25 but is unnecessary
  here and complicates error reporting; prefer explicit driver-side batches.
- Community Edition has a single user database (`neo4j`). Multi-tenancy, if ever needed,
  must be done with labels or properties — not separate databases.

